 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index built per kiosk from each validated snapshot — no service to run, no runtime network dependency, and lookups are plain indexed reads. See Decisions & Alternatives for why the networked directory and managed search options were rejected.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Import runs in stages so that a failure at any stage leaves the previous snapshot active:
+
+1. **Validate.** Verify the signature using the bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness.
+2. **Build.** Materialize the validated records into a new SQLite index file in a staging location beside the active index. Each snapshot gets its own index file; records are never merged into the current file.
+3. **Activate.** Atomically rename the staged index file into place as the active index, then retire the previously active index file. Renaming a complete file is the only activation step, so no partially imported records are ever visible.
+
+Because the staged index is built offline from the snapshot file, activation is a single filesystem operation and a failed import discards only the staging file.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. The lookup reads the active SQLite index — a single indexed query on the normalized serial column — and returns the snapshot date stored in the index's metadata row alongside the records, so the result carries the date of the snapshot actually queried even when no matching record exists. Lookups in progress while a new index is being staged continue against the previous index until activation completes.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index per kiosk, rebuilt from each validated snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory service; managed search service | Lookups are predictable indexed reads using only files on the kiosk, satisfying the no-runtime-network tenet without a timeout or error path. The HTTPS directory makes registry lookups depend on lab network reachability, and a managed service adds recurring cost, credentials, and external connectivity — both introduce failure modes the tenet forbids. The cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on snapshot arrival, which stays inside this node. |
+| Index-per-snapshot, activated by rename | Each snapshot builds a new index file; activation is an atomic rename | In-place updates to a single index database | A single file updated in place exposes partially imported records or requires transactional rollback logic inside the index; per-snapshot files make rollback "keep the old file" and make REG-003/REG-004 structural rather than procedural. Accepts rebuild work per import, which is bounded by the 150 MB snapshot limit. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index; the certificate interface is unchanged by the selection.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Index schema details (column types, exact metadata row) settle at implementation time against the snapshot schema version, not here.
+2. Retention policy for old retired index files (keep only the previous one vs. keep several for rollback) — decide during implementation if disk space on kiosks proves tight.
